values ('10102a79-cbe4-52af-9c7c-2d6f0818f06f', 'rg3-authors-purpose-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-authors-purpose'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How a Spider Spins a Web$q$, $q$A garden spider does not need tools to build its home. It makes what it needs from silk. The silk comes out of tiny parts at the back of the spider's body called spinnerets.

When the silk first comes out, it is a liquid. As the spider pulls it, the silk turns into a thin, strong thread. To start a web, the spider lets one long thread float out on the breeze. The wind carries the thread until it sticks to a branch. This makes a bridge.

Next, the spider builds a frame around the edges. Then it adds threads that go from the middle out to the frame, like the spokes on a bike wheel. These threads are not sticky, so the spider can walk on them.

Last, the spider winds a sticky thread round and round in a spiral. When a fly or moth flies into the web, it gets stuck. The spider feels the web shake and hurries over to catch its meal. Many garden spiders eat their old web and spin a new one almost every day.$q$, 'draft')
